WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.135
A software execution model estimates the
resources a scenario demands without competition

00:00:05.135 --> 00:00:10.154
from other requests. A queueing model adds that
competition. A service station contains

00:00:10.154 --> 00:00:15.288
waiting work and one or more servers, where a
server might represent a processor, disk or

00:00:15.288 --> 00:00:17.884
network link rather than a physical computer.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.108
Residence time includes both waiting and
service; service time measures the resource work

00:00:23.108 --> 00:00:23.452
alone.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:29.263
Kendall notation records assumptions about
arrivals, service times, server count, capacity,

00:00:29.263 --> 00:00:34.963
population and scheduling. In the slide example,
total capacity twenty includes three jobs

00:00:34.963 --> 00:00:40.410
in service and seventeen waiting. State this
convention explicitly because “buffer” is

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:41.043
ambiguous.

00:00:41.043 --> 00:00:46.107
Model choices matter: bursty arrivals, finite
populations and priority scheduling can

00:00:46.107 --> 00:00:51.529
produce different behavior even when average
service demand is unchanged. A useful model is

00:00:51.529 --> 00:00:56.831
precise about its boundary and assumptions. Does
a two-millisecond service demand imply a

00:00:56.831 --> 00:00:59.095
two-millisecond response at high load?

00:00:59.095 --> 00:01:04.431
No. Waiting behind other work adds residence
time even if the service demand is unchanged.

00:01:04.595 --> 00:01:09.904
Start with the observation window, not with a
queueing formula. In twenty seconds, eight

00:01:09.904 --> 00:01:15.333
jobs complete. Divide completions by elapsed
time: eight over twenty gives a throughput of

00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:17.263
zero point four jobs per second.

00:01:17.263 --> 00:01:22.355
The device was busy for sixteen of those twenty
seconds. Its utilization is therefore

00:01:22.355 --> 00:01:27.566
sixteen over twenty, or zero point eight. Notice
the units: throughput is a rate, while

00:01:27.566 --> 00:01:30.081
utilization is a fraction of elapsed time.

00:01:30.263 --> 00:01:35.552
Under the measurement and accounting
assumptions, divide utilization by throughput to
obtain

00:01:35.552 --> 00:01:40.840
service demand. Zero point eight divided by zero
point four gives two seconds per job. Check

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.842
the result in the other direction: throughput
times service demand returns utilization.

00:01:45.842 --> 00:01:49.750
Residence time also includes waiting, so it is a
different quantity.

00:01:49.763 --> 00:01:55.272
The utilization law connects throughput and
service demand, U=X×S for a single service

00:01:55.272 --> 00:02:00.845
resource. Little’s law relates average jobs
inside a boundary to throughput and average

00:02:00.845 --> 00:02:06.738
residence time, N=X×R. Use consistent boundaries
and a stable observation regime; do not mix

00:02:06.738 --> 00:02:09.621
system throughput with a device queue length.

00:02:09.763 --> 00:02:16.518
The NFS exercise completes 10,800 requests in
1,800 seconds, giving six per second; three

00:02:16.518 --> 00:02:23.349
active requests imply average residence of half
a second. A single encoding device is busy

00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:30.331
40% of the time at 800 frames/s. A firmware
change reduces mean service per frame to 0.3 ms.

00:02:30.331 --> 00:02:38.577
What mean service time did the measurements
imply before the change, and what utilization is

00:02:38.577 --> 00:02:46.195
predicted afterward? Before: S=U/X=.40/800=.0005
seconds, or 0.5 ms per frame. After:

00:02:46.195 --> 00:02:54.172
U=800×.0003=.24, or 24%, assuming the throughput
and device boundary stay the same. Lower

00:02:54.172 --> 00:03:00.087
utilization alone does not establish an end-to-
end latency target.

00:03:00.331 --> 00:03:07.884
The M/M/1 model assumes Poisson arrivals,
independent exponentially distributed service

00:03:07.884 --> 00:03:15.524
times and one server with an unbounded waiting
room. In steady state it requires arrival

00:03:15.524 --> 00:03:23.337
rate λ below service rate μ. Its utilization is
ρ=λ/μ, mean residence time is 1/(μ−λ), and

00:03:23.337 --> 00:03:25.160
mean jobs is ρ/(1−ρ).

00:03:25.331 --> 00:03:30.610
These expressions describe long-run averages
under the model, not deterministic limits on

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:31.796
individual requests.

00:03:31.831 --> 00:03:38.269
Birth–death balance explains the geometric state
distribution. At an empty system, departure

00:03:38.269 --> 00:03:44.357
rate is zero; at nonempty states it is μ.
Multiple servers change departure capacity to

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:49.325
min(n,m)μ and require a different waiting
calculation such as Erlang C.

00:03:49.331 --> 00:03:54.519
Finite buffers change the model again: an
infinite-queue tail probability is not

00:03:54.519 --> 00:04:00.162
automatically the exact blocking probability of
a bounded system. Always define whether

00:04:00.162 --> 00:04:06.128
capacity includes the job being served. What
happens to M/M/1 residence time as λ approaches

00:04:06.128 --> 00:04:06.971
μ from below?

00:04:06.971 --> 00:04:12.215
It grows without bound in the model; the stable
formula cannot be used at or above capacity.
